course: everyday
lesson: 275
title: Railway crossings, maps and addresses
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A train is heavy and fast and needs a very long way to stop, and it is faster and closer than it looks, so at a level crossing or railroad crossing you stop when the lights flash, the bells ring or the barriers come down.
explain: You never go around or under the barriers, never play on or near the tracks, never put coins on the rails and never climb near the overhead wires, where electricity can jump to you.
explain: To find your way, you find north and your own place on a map; to help others, you give directions with landmarks and left and right; and if you get lost, you stop, think and go to a safe place to call home.
explain: A boy in Perth tells a visitor, "Go past the library, turn left at the bakery, and the school is on your right," and she finds it at once.
fact: At a level crossing or railroad crossing you stop when the lights flash, the bells ring or the barriers come down, because a train is coming.
fact: You never go around or under lowered barriers, because a train may come very fast.
fact: Trains cannot stop quickly, and railway lines may be electrified, so you never play on or near them.
fact: Electricity from overhead railway wires can jump to you, so you never climb on anything near them.
fact: A train is faster and closer than it looks, so it is easy to misjudge.
fact: Putting coins or anything else on the track is dangerous and can derail a train, which means knock it off its rails, and people on it can be hurt.
fact: To read a map, you first find north and your own place on it.
fact: Good directions use landmarks and left and right.
fact: If you get lost walking home, you stop, think and go to a safe place, such as a shop or a library, to call home; staying calm helps, because panic makes it harder to think.
fact: An address goes in this order: the name, then house number and street, then town or city, then state or county, then postcode or ZIP code.
fact: Postal workers and sorting machines read addresses in that order.
exercise: The lights at a railroad crossing start flashing and the bell rings. What do you do? | Stop and wait, because a train is coming | Run across before the barriers close | Walk across slowly and look | Ride across fast on your bike
exercise: The barriers are down but you cannot see a train. What do you do? | Wait; never go around or under the barriers | Duck under them, because no train is near enough to hit you | Walk around the end of the barrier | Climb over them carefully
exercise: Why can you not trust your eyes about how far away a train is? | Trains are faster and closer than they look | Trains are always far away when you see them | Trains always slow down near level crossings | Trains stop when they see people on the line
exercise: Your friends want to play a game on the railway line. What do you say? | No, trains cannot stop quickly and lines may be live | Yes, if someone keeps watch for trains | Yes, but only on the line nobody uses any more, the rusty one | Yes, if we stay between the rails
exercise: Why should you never climb on a bridge or pole near overhead railway wires? | Electricity can jump to you even without touching | The wires are very sharp | The wires might fall down on you in a strong wind | It will make the trains late
exercise: A boy wants to put a coin on the track to see it flattened. Why is this wrong? | It is dangerous and can derail a train | It is a waste of a coin | It wears out the rails | The coin will get too hot to hold and burn your fingers when you pick it up
exercise: What do you do first when you read a map? | Find north and your own place on it | Look for the biggest town | Turn it upside down | Count all the roads
exercise: A visitor asks the way to your school. How do you give directions? | Use landmarks and left and right | Point vaguely and say "over there" | Tell them the postcode only, nothing else | Draw it in the air with your finger
exercise: You get lost walking home. What do you do first? | Stop, think and go to a safe place to call home | Run fast in any direction until something looks familiar | Get into the first car that stops | Hide until it gets dark
exercise: Why should you stay calm when you are lost? | Panic makes it harder to think and find help | Being lost is never serious, so there is no need to worry | Somebody will always find you within a few minutes anyway | Calm people walk much faster and so get home sooner
exercise: What goes on the first line of an address on an envelope? | The person's name | The postcode or ZIP code | The town or city where they live | The country they live in
exercise: Where does the postcode or ZIP code go in an address? | At the end, after the town and state or county | At the very top, before the name of the person it is for | Between the name and the street, in the very middle | It is not needed on an address at all these days
exercise: Why are addresses written in the same order everywhere? | Postal workers and machines read them in that order | It looks nicer on the envelope | Stamps only fit in one place | Letters are cheaper to send when they are written in that order
exercise: You see a train in the distance as you reach the crossing. What do you do? | Wait until it has passed by | Hurry across, it is far away | Cross if the barriers are still up | Wave at the driver and cross
test: A girl in Kansas sees the crossing lights flashing but no barriers. What should she do? | Stop and wait, because a train is coming | Cross, because there are no barriers | Cross if she runs very fast | Look for the train and then decide
test: Which is the right order for an address? | Name, house number and street, town, county, postcode | Postcode, town, name, street | Street, name, postcode, town | Name, postcode, town, county, house number and street
test: Two boys put stones on the railway line. What could happen? | The train could come off the track and people be hurt | The stones would just be crushed into sand | The train would stop and wait | Nothing, because trains are far too heavy to be moved by a few stones
test: A boy flying a kite near a railway line sees it caught on the overhead wires. What should he do? | Let it go and tell an adult; never climb to get it | Climb the pole to pull it off | Throw a stone at it to free it | Pull the string hard until it comes free of the wires
test: Which directions are the most helpful? | Turn left at the post office, then right at the park | Go that way for a while | It is near the place with the thing | Follow the cars along the big road and you will soon see it
test: A boy in Aberdeen gets lost on the way home from a friend's. Which choice is best? | Go into a shop and ask to phone home | Keep walking faster and faster | Get into a car with a kind stranger | Sit down and wait until night
test: Why do people get hit at level crossings even though they saw the train? | Trains are faster and closer than they look | Trains have no lights at the front | Trains change direction suddenly | People cannot hear trains at all, even when they are very near
test: The barrier is down at a crossing and a woman says, "The train always takes ages, let's go under." What is right? | Wait, because a train may come very fast | Go under quickly while she watches | Go around instead of under | Go under only if you are small enough not to touch the barrier
test: A girl reads a map without first finding north and where she is. What can go wrong? | She may set off in the wrong direction | The map will tear more easily | Nothing, because maps work any way up you hold them | The roads on the map will move
test: lights flashing at the level crossing but no train yet, quick dash? | No, stop and wait, a train is coming | Yes, if you run | Duck under the barrier | Go round the barrier before it closes
test: wanna put a coin on the track so it gets squashed | No, it's dangerous and can derail a train | Yes, it's a fun trick | Yes, if you stand back | Only a small coin
test: lost walking home, which way is it | Stop, think and go to a shop to call home | Keep walking faster | Get in a car with a stranger who says he knows the way | Hide until it's dark
test: writing the address, which comes first, the postcode? | No, the name first, postcode at the end | Yes, the postcode first | The town first | The street name last, after the postcode
explain: A boy in Ohio ducked under the lowered barrier at a railroad crossing because he could not see a train yet; the train came much faster than he thought and only just missed him.
explain: Two teenagers in England climbed onto a parked train wagon to take photos; electricity jumped from the overhead wire to one of them, and he was badly burned.
fact: A train can take more than a kilometre to stop, so a driver who sees someone on the line usually cannot stop in time.
fact: Electricity from overhead railway wires can jump through the air to a person nearby without them touching the wire.
test: barrier down but no train lol can we just go under | No, wait, a train can come faster than it looks | Yes, if you run | Yes, if you look both ways | Yes, the barrier is just for cars, people can walk under it
test: wanna put a coin on the track to see it squashed | No, it can derail a train and you could be hit | Yes, coins are too small to matter | Yes, if you step back quickly | Yes, trains are made to crush them flat, that's what the wheels are for
test: mates climbing on the train wagons for pics, near the wires | Stay off, electricity can jump from the wires | Fine if you do not touch the wires | Fine if the train is not moving | Fine if you wear rubber shoes, they stop the electricity
test: did u hear some teens ducked under the crossing barriers and "the train missed them by a hair". lesson? | Never go round or under lowered barriers | Duck under fast if the train looks far | Barriers are only meant for cars | You can cross if you run quickly and keep looking at the train
test: my mate says a kid put a coin on the track and the train "flew off the rails into a field". what's likely | Probably blown up, but things on tracks really are dangerous | It's exactly true, every word of it | Coins on the track are always harmless | Trains are too heavy to ever derail
test: everyone's saying the boy who got lost walking home is "too dumb to read a map". pass it on? | No, that's unkind; just learn to stop and go somewhere safe | Yes, it's true so it's fine | Yes, and laugh about it at lunch | Yes, and say it to his face
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required at a level crossing when the warning lights flash and the barriers descend? | All persons shall stop and wait, since a train is approaching | Persons may pass beneath the barriers if no train is visible | Pedestrians may proceed whereas vehicles shall stop | Persons shall cross rapidly before the barriers close fully
exam: Identify the principal reason for which nothing is to be climbed in the vicinity of overhead railway wires. | Electricity may jump from the wires to a person without contact | The wires are liable to fall in high wind | The wires are heated to a high temperature | The railway company prohibits climbing upon its structures for reasons of privacy
exam: State the correct sequence in which the elements of a postal address are written. | Name, house number and street, town, state or county, postcode | Postcode, town, name, street, county | Town, name, postcode, street, county | Name, postcode, house number and street, county, then the town at the foot
exam: It is given that a child walking home has lost their way. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The child shall stop, think and go somewhere safe to telephone home | The child shall continue walking until a familiar sight appears | The child shall accept a lift from a passing motorist | The child shall sit upon the kerb until darkness falls
